body:
Resolves #1387

## What

Content pictures whose `wp-image-N` class names an attachment that does not own the file in their `src` (blocks from imported templates keep the template's ID) now get what core gives every other picture, from the attachment that does own it: width and height, `srcset` and `sizes`, and core's loading attributes.

`SEOProStack_Image_Loading::own_sizes()` (`includes/features/class-seoprostack-image-loading.php`), a `wp_content_img_tag` filter at 5, before Media storage (10) and `content_image()` (20):

- acts only on a tag core gave an ID but no `srcset`, whose `src` is in this site's uploads and is not an SVG;
- leaves it alone when the named attachment's file, `original_image` or one of its sizes is that file (core has decided);
- otherwise finds the owner with `attachment_url_to_postid()` (as given, without a `-WxH` suffix, then with `-scaled`), checks it owns the file, once per file a request;
- runs core's `wp_img_tag_add_width_and_height_attr()`, `wp_img_tag_add_srcset_and_sizes_attr()`, `wp_img_tag_add_loading_optimization_attrs()` (only when it added width and height: core skips and does not count pictures without them) and `wp_img_tag_add_auto_sizes()`, in core's order, guarded for WordPress 6.2;
- passes the owner to Media storage's `content_image()` when that is hooked;
- leaves nested Post Content passes to the outer one, as `content_image()` does.

Kadence Blocks backgrounds had the same gap. `SEOProStack_Hero_Picture::get()` (`includes/class-seoprostack-hero-picture.php`) looked a picture up by address (`url_id()`, GH#1347) only when the block saved no ID. Now:

- it also looks the address up when the saved ID's metadata lacks the address's file (new `sized()`);
- `url_id()` also tries a size copy's address without its `-WxH` suffix;
- the attachment found must have the file.

This covers the row a page opens with, rows and columns further down, row background sliders and Advanced Slider slides.

## Why

On one Kadence site (7 November 2026) all six home-page pictures named attachment 2864, which does not exist. Core added nothing, and phones fetched 1365 to 2048 px files shown about 126 px wide: 675 KB of pictures before the largest paint; PageSpeed mobile 79, LCP 5.7 s. The largest paint itself, a Section background saving the same ID, got no copies either (`seoprostackRows(…, 0, 1)` in the page).

## Verification

- Test site (`scripts/test-site.sh up`, WordPress 7.1.4, PHP 8.3), one page with eight cases:
  1. missing ID, original file: width 1600, height 1200, `srcset` 300w to 1600w, `sizes`, `fetchpriority="high"` (core's first large picture), `data-no-lazy`;
  2. another attachment's ID, large copy: width 1024, height 768 and `srcset` from the right attachment;
  3. right ID: unchanged (core's own);
  4. SVG, missing ID: unchanged;
  5. external address, missing ID: unchanged;
  6. right ID, lazy: unchanged (core's `sizes="auto, …"`);
  7. missing ID, sized in markup, lazy: `srcset` and `sizes="auto, (max-width: 1600px) 100vw, 1600px"`;
  8. missing ID, file not an attachment: unchanged.
  PHP log empty.
- Same site with Kadence Blocks: a row with three Sections whose background names attachment A's file with a missing ID, attachment B's ID and A's ID. All three get A's copies (300 to 1600). `get()` direct: missing ID, other ID and no ID give 1600×1200 with copies 300, 768, 1024, 1536 and 1600; other ID with the large copy's address gives 1024×768 with copies 300, 768 and 1024; an external address gives null. PHP log empty.
- Read-only check on the live site's data (same lookup, core's functions): the five home-page files resolve to attachments 37, 38, 39, 15 and 14, each with 200 or 300 px copies upward.
- `bash scripts/lint.sh php phpcs phpstan`: all passed.
- Preview site: not used. The change adds only the attributes core adds to correctly named pictures, so it looks the same in light and dark mode, and the preview site has no such pictures.

